{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789292751,"nonce":"9DqUd0w8EDQJii+Srp6I16HU8PA=","title":"Python: relative imports and packages with __init__.py","message":"The module linker resolves `a.b` to `a/b.py` and nothing else. Not\nhandled: `from . import x` and `from .mod import y` (relative to the\nimporting module's package), `pkg/__init__.py` running on `import pkg`\nand providing `pkg.name`, `__all__`, and a module used as a value\n(`m = importlib.import_module(...)`, passing a module around). Relative\nimports need the linker to know each module's package path; __init__\nneeds the linker to load `pkg/__init__.py` as module `pkg` when a file\nby that name exists.","files":null}]}